WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
Artificial intelligence is already in almost every

2
00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:03.100
major company.

3
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.240
It solves math problems and generates hundreds

4
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.400
of millions in revenue.

5
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.540
But there’s one detail:

6
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.620
the model alone isn’t doing that work.

7
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.120
The model is like a brain in

8
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:11.600
a jar.

9
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:12.320
On its own,

10
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.880
it can’t speak or act.

11
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.460
The real work is done by everything

12
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:16.920
around it, and that has a name:

13
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:17.740
the harness.

14
00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.620
In this video,

15
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.020
I’ll explain what a harness is and

16
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.080
its five parts.

17
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.180
Let’s start with what you already know:

18
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.380
Claude Code and Codex.

19
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.340
Neither one is an AI model.

20
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:28.440
They’re both harnesses.

21
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.840
Claude Code is Anthropic’s harness for the

22
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.560
Claude family, with models like Fable,

23
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.400
Opus, Sonnet, and Haiku.

24
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.820
Codex is OpenAI’s harness for the GPT

25
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:37.300
family.

26
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.440
Both let AI work,

27
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:39.220
not just chat.

28
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:40.780
And here’s something interesting:

29
00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.260
take the same model and put it

30
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.220
in two different harnesses,

31
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:44.720
and the results can change a lot.

32
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.300
Even a chat window is a harness,

33
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.160
just a simpler one built for conversation.

34
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.500
Much of the progress we see in

35
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.240
AI comes from better harnesses,

36
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.360
not just smarter models.

37
00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:53.880
So when you choose an AI tool,

38
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.040
you also choose its harness.

39
00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.380
Now look at the model on its

40
00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:56.640
own.

41
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.480
A language model is trained to predict

42
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.600
text: a piece of text goes in,

43
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.560
and a slightly longer piece comes out.

44
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.840
You type “the sky is,” and it

45
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.800
completes the sentence with “blue,” because it

46
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.500
has read that phrase billions of times.

47
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.340
On its own,

48
00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.280
it has four limits.

49
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.480
It has no memory:

50
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.080
every new chat starts empty.

51
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.000
It can’t see:

52
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.300
it can’t see your files or your

53
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:15.720
screen.

54
00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:16.620
It can’t act:

55
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:17.860
it can only tell you what to

56
00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:18.140
do.

57
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.200
And it can’t check:

58
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.720
it doesn’t know whether the code it

59
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:21.320
wrote works.

60
00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.060
At its core,

61
00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.600
the model predicts the next piece of

62
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:24.740
text, over and over.

63
00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.160
Think of the model as a horse.

64
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.100
It has a lot of power,

65
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:27.940
but no control:

66
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:29.500
sometimes it responds brilliantly,

67
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.660
sometimes strangely.

68
00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.380
The harness is the saddle and reins.

69
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:33.960
It directs all that power toward a

70
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:34.820
useful goal.

71
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.600
A harness has five parts around the

72
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.040
model.

73
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.560
Context tells it what you’re doing and

74
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:38.980
why.

75
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.760
Memory keeps track of what’s happened.

76
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:42.540
Tools let the model take action.

77
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.400
Verification checks whether it did the job

78
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:44.760
right.

79
00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.040
And permissions prevent things you don’t want.

80
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.720
Today, a good harness is as valuable

81
00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:49.500
as a good model.

82
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.060
The first part is context.

83
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.260
When a session starts,

84
00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.240
the model may be smart,

85
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.780
but it knows nothing about you.

86
00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.780
Context answers: what’s the project,

87
00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:57.880
how are the files organized,

88
00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.180
what changed most recently,

89
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.380
and what tools does it have?

90
00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:01.800
The harness puts all of that at

91
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:02.680
the start of the conversation,

92
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:03.600
behind the scenes,

93
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.100
for the model to read first.

94
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.620
In Claude Code and Codex,

95
00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.100
that includes a map of the code,

96
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.580
so it can find the right file

97
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:10.540
and ignore the rest,

98
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.160
and files like CLAUDE.md and

99
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.020
AGENTS.md, with rules such as:

100
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.400
use our coding style,

101
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.680
don’t touch this folder,

102
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:18.740
always run the tests.

103
00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.560
The AI reads this every session,

104
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.480
and you write it once.

105
00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:22.900
The second part is memory.

106
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:23.840
The more you talk,

107
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.620
the more the context window fills up.

108
00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.200
And a full window is a problem:

109
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.960
the model gets less capable and starts

110
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.880
seeing instructions that contradict each other.

111
00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.420
The solution is called compaction.

112
00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:33.860
The harness keeps what matters:

113
00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:34.920
the goal, the plan,

114
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:35.840
and the changed files.

115
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.080
It shrinks old steps into a short

116
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:37.480
summary.

117
00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:38.800
And it removes failed attempts.

118
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.260
That leaves room to keep working in

119
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.090
the same direction.

120
00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.840
Claude Code does this automatically when the

121
00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:43.880
window is nearly full.

122
00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.600
Many harnesses also keep a record of

123
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:46.580
changes to each file,

124
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.420
like Git, instead of storing the whole

125
00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:49.020
file.

126
00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.220
The third part is tools.

127
00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.900
The model only produces text.

128
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.580
So the harness watches for a special

129
00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:54.380
kind of text:

130
00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:55.440
a tool request.

131
00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.920
This is called tool calling.

132
00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.320
The model makes a request,

133
00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.520
the harness carries it out—whether that’s reading

134
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.200
a file, searching for files,

135
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.080
running a program,

136
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.060
or editing a file—and the result goes

137
00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:05.860
back to the model.

138
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.840
This happens dozens of times a minute.

139
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.200
Harnesses have come a long way here.

140
00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.360
For example, instead of rewriting an entire

141
00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.320
file, the AI changes only the line

142
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:13.980
it needs to.

143
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.140
That means fewer tokens and fewer errors.

144
00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.080
And each request follows a fixed format,

145
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:18.840
called a schema.

146
00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.900
The best-known format for connecting other apps

147
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.060
is MCP.

148
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:23.100
Anthropic created it,

149
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.700
and OpenAI uses it too.

150
00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.180
You can even ask your AI to

151
00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:27.620
create an MCP server.

152
00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.140
The fourth part is verification:

153
00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.000
checking the work.

154
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.400
Without it, the model looks at the

155
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.060
code and says,

156
00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.980
“Looks good to me.” But it doesn’t

157
00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.160
know whether the code runs.

158
00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.540
Remember when ChatGPT said seventeen times three

159
00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:38.320
was forty-eight?

160
00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:39.620
The solution was to give it a

161
00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:40.280
calculator.

162
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:41.460
Code works the same way:

163
00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.040
the harness runs it and gives the

164
00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.620
actual result back for the AI to

165
00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:45.300
read.

166
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.160
That’s what tests are for.

167
00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:47.660
They tell you pass or fail,

168
00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:48.920
without guessing.

169
00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.500
Linters find mistakes in the code.

170
00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:52.500
And formatters make everything follow the same

171
00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:53.040
style.

172
00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.460
Then there’s the exit code:

173
00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:55.580
zero means success;

174
00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.500
any other number means an error.

175
00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.000
The fifth part is permissions.

176
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.900
The harness checks every action before carrying

177
00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:01.420
it out.

178
00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:02.580
Editing a file is fine.

179
00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:03.920
But deleting files,

180
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.140
changing system settings,

181
00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.920
or sending data to an unfamiliar website

182
00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.540
requires your approval.

183
00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:09.520
A question pops up:

184
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.460
“Can I delete the old folder?” And

185
00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:12.500
you decide what needs approval.

186
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.080
This is handled with hooks,

187
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:15.640
which are rules that run at specific

188
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:16.220
points.

189
00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.480
For example: run the tests after every

190
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:18.820
edit.

191
00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.720
Or: never allow this command.

192
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.680
There are also checkpoints for undoing changes,

193
00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:23.720
and a sandbox:

194
00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.040
an isolated environment with limits on time,

195
00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.760
memory, and internet access.

196
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.280
If the AI makes a mistake,

197
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.800
the damage stays inside.

198
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:31.800
Codex, for example,

199
00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.360
uses a fresh computer in the cloud

200
00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.520
for every task.

201
00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:35.960
So where does the model fit into

202
00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:36.400
all this?

203
00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:37.920
It’s the one making decisions.

204
00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.060
It gets the context and current state,

205
00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:41.340
then chooses the next step.

206
00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.260
Some models think before they answer,

207
00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:44.960
and that reasoning is also part of

208
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:45.400
the harness.

209
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.440
Here’s a simple trick:

210
00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.000
the model writes down an idea,

211
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.340
and the harness gives it back with

212
00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:51.280
the word “but” at the end.

213
00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:52.800
That forces the model to look for

214
00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.880
exceptions and review what it said.

215
00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.760
Then the harness organizes the output,

216
00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.060
which might be more thinking,

217
00:04:58.060 --> 00:04:58.980
a tool request,

218
00:04:58.980 --> 00:04:59.940
or a message for you.

219
00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.660
The harness doesn’t make the model smarter.

220
00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.060
It makes the model more useful.

221
00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:03.900
Put it all together,

222
00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.000
and you get the agent loop.

223
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.620
First, it thinks and decides what to

224
00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:07.060
do next.

225
00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.960
Then it acts by requesting a tool.

226
00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.200
Permissions check whether it’s allowed.

227
00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:11.060
The action runs,

228
00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.220
ideally in a sandbox.

229
00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:13.580
The result is trimmed down to keep

230
00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:14.540
only what’s useful.

231
00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:16.440
Then the agent reads what happened and

232
00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:16.780
why.

233
00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:17.780
And it thinks again.

234
00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:19.400
This loop repeats until the goal is

235
00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:19.680
met.

236
00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:20.300
At the end,

237
00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.540
the agent tells you what changed.

238
00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:22.400
It’s called ReAct,

239
00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.060
from “reason” and “act,” and it’s behind

240
00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.080
most agents today.

241
00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:25.980
So what’s next?

242
00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.960
First, MCP in more and more apps,

243
00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.560
so any AI can connect to them.

244
00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:31.000
Second, teams of agents,

245
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.140
where one plans,

246
00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:33.780
another builds, another reviews,

247
00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:34.820
and another tests.

248
00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.900
Third, running several versions of the same

249
00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:38.660
task at once and keeping only the

250
00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:39.740
one that passes the tests.

251
00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:42.080
And fourth, harnesses that improve themselves:

252
00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.380
they look at past mistakes and create

253
00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:46.780
better rules, catching failures earlier and with

254
00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:47.580
less work from people.

255
00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:48.700
And the best part:

256
00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.260
a harness is software.

257
00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:51.240
Anyone

258
00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:51.860
can contribute.

259
00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.520
If you want to learn to use

260
00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:54.380
Claude Code, Codex,

261
00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:55.120
and agents in practice,

262
00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.947
the projects and courses are free at

263
00:05:56.947 --> 00:05:57.720
INEMA dot club.
